<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azare</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azare is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi-state">Bauchi State</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It became the seat of the Katagum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>, founded during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a> of the early 19th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Katagum still lives there. It is a trading centre for grain and livestock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azare.jpg" alt="Azare" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Musaddam Idriss · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azare_Town_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded by Mallam Zaki, who had been appointed by Shehu Usman dan Fodio. His father had first settled at Nafada in the Gombe emirate and later moved to Yayu, where Mallam Zaki was born. After his father's death Mallam Zaki went to Usman dan Fodio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> to take his father's place, and in about 1809 he settled at Tashena, a village about 90 kilometres from present-day Azare.</p><p>Mallam Zaki was succeeded by his brother Sulaiman Adandaya, who ruled from 1814 to 1816, and the third emir, Mallam Dankauwa, ruled from 1816 until he resigned in 1846. The town belongs to the historical Katagum Emirate, which covers the whole of Bauchi North. In the 19th century the emirate drew immigrants who came to trade, and Islamic orders such as the Tijjaniyyah and Qadiriyyah were present there from that time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Azare lies at an elevation of 436 metres on the Azare Katagum plain, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> savannah, and is the largest of a group of nearby towns that includes Jama'are, Misau and Bulkachuwa. The dry season lasts from October to April and the rains fall from May to September. The flood plains, or fadama, are fertile, and most of the people live by farming and herding. The main crops are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, late millet, groundnut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and guinea corn, with groundnut and cotton the chief export crops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population grew from 69,035 at the 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> to an estimated 110,452 in 2007. Most inhabitants are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> and Muslim, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> is widely spoken. The town has the Federal Medical Centre, Azare, the Federal Government College Azare and the College of Education Azare, and an airstrip about 10 kilometres to the south-east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azare” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirate's seat later moved to <b>Azare</b>, where the Emir of Katagum still reigns in Bauchi State.</blockquote><a href="/state/katagum-emirate">Katagum Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
